window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-25d5929fb05b9f65"]={"key":"ny_main-25d5929fb05b9f65","group":"ny_main","uid":"25d5929fb05b9f65","rank":20,"title":"anvilcross medical transfer decision memorandum","occupation":"police_detective_supervisors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You're the first-line supervisor at Anvilcross Regional Office, and I need a decision memorandum ready by 15:00 Tuesday so Nadia Lindqvist can approve police support by 10:00 Wednesday for Harrowgate Services’ 14:00 transfer of one violent detainee to an off-site diagnostic clinic. Farida Gasparyan will use the memorandum to brief the transport team, so it must support an operational decision rather than read like a general policy summary. Use the attached `anvilcross_custody_medical_transfer_policy.pdf` as the only supplied policy authority for this review. The current case packet records a detainee with a violent offense and two documented assaults while detained, but no current credible escape plan, no weapon access, and a medically stable condition at departure; the diagnostic visit is nevertheless required. The clinic is a public-facing outpatient site with no dedicated secure holding room, and the appointment is during normal operating hours. The supplied policy distinguishes mandatory staffing from recommended enhanced coverage, but it is not obvious how that boundary applies to this profile. It also treats the rare situation in which a medical collapse occurs during an armed escape attempt as a potentially fatal emergency, and that situation must not be blurred into the ordinary transfer recommendation. Please prepare `anvilcross_medical_transfer_decision_memo.pptx` and write it into the top level of the working directory, using exactly that filename. Keep the presentation to no more than seven slides, with all material needed by the decision maker on the slides rather than hidden in an appendix or speaker notes. Please make the memorandum cover these requirements in a clear numbered sequence within the presentation: 1) identify the decision Nadia must make, the Wednesday operating deadline, and the audience Farida is briefing; 2) state the policy’s effective or as-of date and cite the relevant policy sections or paragraphs for material conclusions; 3) compare every escort and clinic-security configuration that the supplied policy makes available, including staffing, custody-control implications, clinic exposure, and operational burden; 4) distinguish what the policy requires from what it recommends, and apply that distinction to the detainee’s actual indicators rather than treating the word “violent” as a complete classification; 5) make one unambiguous recommendation for Wednesday, with the personnel roles, clinic handoff arrangement, timing, and accountable owner concrete enough for the transport team to act without asking the author what was intended; 6) address the medical-collapse-during-armed-escape situation in a visibly separate treatment, including the policy boundary between that emergency and a routine medical problem, and make clear what the policy does not itself authorize or establish; 7) identify any material assumption or unresolved operational dependency without inventing facts, medical judgments, legal powers, costs, or policy thresholds that are not in the case packet or the attached policy; and 8) keep the terminology, staffing description, recommendation, and closing decision consistent throughout. This is a doctrinal memorandum, so write the policy position precisely and pair each controlling rule with its limit rather than overstating a precaution as a command. End with the decision itself, not a generic list of future steps.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/anvilcross_custody_medical_transfer_policy.pdf` — The sole governing policy source for custody-transfer minimums, enhanced escort guidance, clinic-security arrangements, policy terminology, effective date, and the exceptional medical-collapse-during-armed-escape provision.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `anvilcross_medical_transfer_decision_memo.pptx` — A concise decision memorandum presentation recommending an executable police-support arrangement for Wednesday's detainee transfer. (Write the file at the top level of the solver's starting working directory using exactly the specified filename. Use no more than seven slides. The deck must stand alone for Nadia Lindqvist and Farida Gasparyan, cite the supplied policy, compare the available options, separate mandatory requirements from recommendations, treat the exceptional armed-escape medical collapse distinctly, and finish with one clear decision.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/anvilcross_custody_medical_transfer_policy.pdf","bytes":7766,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/7d258348d5fc5ca851519051cbe2acdc47e6e8c0804c486c5ea568bb0cfe3e5e.pdf","sha256":"7d258348d5fc5ca851519051cbe2acdc47e6e8c0804c486c5ea568bb0cfe3e5e","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"ANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE                                                                                                     CRO-CMT-01\n\n\n\n\n         Custody Medical Transfer and Clinic Security Policy\nPolicy ID CRO-CMT-01 | Effective/as-of 1 February 2025 | Custody Operations\n\n  APPLIES TO                   Escorted off-site diagnostic or medical transfers from custody\n\n  SOURCE STATUS                Operational policy; neutral configuration framework\n\n  MODAL KEY                    shall / must = binding within this policy; should / may consider = supervisory recommendation\n\n\nThis policy uses a mandatory floor and a discretionary enhancement path. The configuration descriptions below are\nplanning options; this policy does not select a configuration for a particular detainee.\n\nSECTION 1 | Purpose and scope\nP1.1 This policy applies to an escorted off-site diagnostic or medical transfer from custody. It establishes custody-transfer\nminimums, planning configurations, and clinic-security controls for the movement.\nP1.2 This policy does not itself expand statutory or common-law authority. External law, medical judgment, and local\noperational facts remain controlling and must not be inferred from this policy.\n\nSECTION 2 | Modal vocabulary\nP2.1 Within this policy, shall and must state binding requirements. Should and may consider state recommendations that\nrequire responsible-supervisor judgment; recommended language is not a binding high-risk classification.\n\nSECTION 3 | Mandatory baseline for an ordinary transfer\nP3.1 For an ordinary off-site custodial medical transfer, the responsible supervisor shall approve the movement and identify\nthe transport team before departure.\nP3.2 The mandatory minimum transport team shall comprise: (a) one transport driver, who operates the vehicle and\nmaintains team communications; and (b) one custody escort, who maintains direct custody control, conducts the identity and\nrestraint check, and leads the controlled movement and handoff.\nP3.3 Before departure, the driver and custody escort must complete and communicate identity, restraint, route, and\ncommunications checks. The policy sets no fixed departure lead time.\n\nP3.4 At the receiving clinic, the custody escort shall retain custody control until a controlled handoff is completed with the\nreceiving-site contact at a designated handoff point or through an equivalent controlled-access measure. The detainee shall\nnot be left unattended in an uncontrolled public area.\nP3.5 The receiving clinic controls clinical care. The custody and clinic-security roles in this policy do not diagnose, direct\ntreatment, or substitute for external medical judgment.\n\nSECTION 4 | Current high-risk triggers and supervisory judgment\nP4.1 A current high-risk trigger exists when one or more of the following is present: (a) a current credible escape plan or\nescape attempt; (b) weapon access; or (c) active medical instability requiring continuous clinical monitoring.\n\nP4.2 Documented past violence, including a violent offense or assaults while detained, is a significant factor for enhanced\nconsideration. Past violence alone does not automatically convert recommended coverage into mandatory high-risk staffing\nwhen no current trigger in P4.1 is present.\nP4.3 When a current high-risk trigger is present, the responsible supervisor shall use the enhanced mobile escort\nconfiguration described in P5.2.\n\nP4.4 When a current high-risk trigger is present and the receiving clinic cannot ensure controlled access, a dedicated\nclinic-security post or equivalent controlled-access measure shall be used.\nP4.5 When no current high-risk trigger is present, the responsible supervisor should consider enhanced mobile escort where\nhistory, behavior, transfer complexity, or the environment indicates a need for additional custody resilience. A dedicated\n\n\nCustody Medical Transfer and Clinic Security Policy | Effective/as-of 1 February 2025                                              Page 1\n\fANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE                                                                                                            CRO-CMT-01\n\n\n\n\nclinic-security post or equivalent controlled-access measure should be considered for a public-facing clinic without a dedicated\nsecure holding room. These measures are recommended, not mandatory, on those facts alone.\n\nSECTION 5 | Available planning configurations\nP5.1 Configuration A - mandatory minimum escort with ordinary controlled clinic handoff. Staffing: one transport driver and\none custody escort. Custody control: the custody escort maintains direct control through the controlled handoff. Clinic\nexposure: no dedicated police clinic post; access relies on the receiving-site arrangement and the controlled handoff.\nOperational burden: lowest, and this is the mandatory minimum for an ordinary transfer.\n\nP5.2 Configuration B - enhanced mobile escort. Staffing: the Configuration A team plus one additional custody escort who\ntravels with the team. Custody control: adds mobile observation and response coverage while the custody escort remains the\ndirect controller. Clinic exposure: the additional escort is mobile, not a dedicated clinic-security post; public-facing clinic\nexposure remains unless the receiving site provides controlled access. Operational burden: additional personnel and\ncoordination. Use is mandatory under P4.3 when a current high-risk trigger is present; otherwise it is a recommended\nenhancement under P4.5.\nP5.3 Configuration C - coordinated enhanced arrangement. Staffing: Configuration B plus one dedicated clinic-security\nofficer/post or equivalent controlled-access measure. Custody control: combines mobile resilience with an access-control and\nclinic-interface function; the custody escort remains responsible for direct custody. Clinic exposure: the post or equivalent\nmeasure addresses controlled access at the public-facing clinic and the absence of a dedicated secure holding room.\nOperational burden: highest of the three because it adds a fixed clinic function and coordination. The clinic-security function is\nmandatory under P4.4 when a current high-risk trigger is present and the clinic cannot ensure controlled access; under P4.5 it\nis recommended, not mandatory, when no current trigger is present.\nP5.4 A clinic-security officer/post is separate from the additional mobile custody escort. Neither role performs clinical care.\nThe policy does not establish staffing availability, cost, or a specific clinic layout.\n\nSECTION 6 | Receiving-site dependency\nP6.1 Before movement, the responsible supervisor or delegate should confirm the receiving-site contact, designated handoff\npoint, and the available clinic-security post or equivalent controlled-access measure when Configuration C is selected. If the\ndependency cannot be confirmed, the supervisor must resolve the operational choice before departure; this policy does not\nprescribe a substitute clinic layout.\n\nSECTION 7 | Separate emergency: medical collapse during an armed escape attempt\nP7.1 A rare combined event in which a medical collapse occurs during an armed escape attempt is treated as a potentially\nfatal emergency. It is separate from ordinary transfer planning.\nP7.2 On recognition, personnel shall immediately interrupt routine transfer activity and clearly communicate both threats: the\nmedical emergency and the armed escape.\nP7.3 Personnel shall seek emergency medical response, take life-safety action consistent with law and training, contain the\nscene, and notify the responsible supervisor or command channel.\n\nP7.4 Ordinary custody movement shall not resume until the responsible supervisor or command channel provides the\npolicy-required clearance after the emergency and threat have been addressed.\nP7.5 An ordinary medical collapse, violent history, or routine transfer alone does not trigger P7.1 and does not make this\nexception the normal transfer staffing rule. P7.1-P7.4 do not independently authorize a search, use of force, diagnosis, or any\nother legal power.\n\nSECTION 8 | Source limitations\nP8.1 For this review, the policy is the supplied policy authority. It does not establish external legal powers, medical diagnoses,\ncosts, staffing availability, or facts about a receiving site beyond what is expressly confirmed. Those matters require the\napplicable external authority, clinical judgment, or local operational confirmation.\n\nControlled document note: This fictional policy is frozen for the supplied review and is the sole policy authority for that review.\n\n\n\n\nCustody Medical Transfer and Clinic Security Policy | Effective/as-of 1 February 2025                                                     Page 2\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/anvilcross_medical_transfer_decision_memo.pptx","bytes":50663,"extension":".pptx","url":"assets/0fc6d975c2779233516e9e4f91af63a52c76b9efc5a4eb90909bdfd7237d5265.pptx","sha256":"0fc6d975c2779233516e9e4f91af63a52c76b9efc5a4eb90909bdfd7237d5265","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"=== 幻灯片 1 ===\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE\n1 | DECISION TO APPROVE\nWednesday medical transfer\nA decision memorandum for one violent detainee, one required diagnostic visit, and one public-facing clinic without dedicated secure holding.\nNadia Lindqvist must approve police support by 10:00 Wednesday for Harrowgate Services' 14:00 Wednesday transfer. The memo is ready by 15:00 Tuesday. Farida Gasparyan is the operational briefing audience and will brief the transport team.\nRECOMMENDATION\nConfiguration C\nCoordinated enhanced arrangement\nEnhanced mobile escort: driver + custody escort + additional custody escort\nDedicated clinic-security post or equivalent controlled-access measure\nCustody escort retains direct custody through controlled handoff\nRECOMMENDED - NOT A BINDING HIGH-RISK MANDATE\nCASE SIGNALS\nViolent offense + two documented assaults while detained\nNo current credible escape plan | no weapon access | medically stable at departure\nPublic-facing outpatient clinic | no dedicated secure holding room | normal operating hours\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE  |  DECISION MEMORANDUM\nSource: CRO-CMT-01 | Effective/as-of 1 February 2025\n01\n\n=== 幻灯片 2 ===\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE\n2 | Policy position: the boundary matters\nThe policy establishes a binding floor, then leaves enhanced coverage to trigger-based rules and supervisory judgment.\nSOURCE\nCRO-CMT-01  |  Effective/as-of 1 February 2025\nSole supplied policy authority for this review\nMODAL LANGUAGE\nshall / must\nBinding requirements within the policy.\nshould / may consider\nRecommendations requiring responsible-supervisor judgment; they do not, by themselves, create a high-risk mandate.\nSource: Sec. 2, P2.1\nMANDATORY BASELINE | ORDINARY OFF-SITE TRANSFER\nThe minimum that must be in place\n01\nAPPROVAL\nResponsible supervisor shall approve the movement and identify the transport team before departure.\n02\nTEAM\nTransport driver + custody escort. Driver operates the vehicle and maintains communications; custody escort maintains direct custody control.\n03\nCHECKS\nBefore departure, driver and custody escort must complete and communicate identity, restraint, route, and communications checks.\n04\nHANDOFF\nCustody escort retains control until a controlled handoff with the receiving-site contact at a designated point or equivalent controlled-access measure.\nThe policy also states that the detainee shall not be left unattended in an uncontrolled public area; the receiving clinic controls clinical care. Source: Sec. 3, P3.1-P3.5\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE  |  DECISION MEMORANDUM\nSource: CRO-CMT-01 | Effective/as-of 1 February 2025\n02\n\n=== 幻灯片 3 ===\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE\n3 | Three available configurations\nEvery option begins with the same custody baseline. The difference is where additional resilience is placed.\nCONFIGURATION\nSTAFFING\nCUSTODY CONTROL + CLINIC EXPOSURE\nOPERATIONAL BURDEN / POLICY STATUS\nA | MINIMUM ESCORT\nMANDATORY MINIMUM\n1 transport driver + 1 custody escort\nDirect custody by the custody escort; identity/restraint/communications checks; controlled clinic handoff.\nNo dedicated police clinic post. Public-clinic access relies on the receiving-site arrangement and the controlled handoff.\nLowest burden. Binding floor for an ordinary transfer. Source: Sec. 3, P3.1-P3.4; Sec. 5, P5.1.\nB | ENHANCED MOBILE ESCORT\nRECOMMENDED ENHANCEMENT\nConfiguration A + 1 additional custody escort traveling with the team\nAdds mobile observation and response coverage; the primary custody escort remains the direct controller.\nThe additional escort is mobile, not a clinic-security post. Public-facing clinic exposure remains unless the receiving site controls access.\nAdded personnel and coordination. Mandatory when a current high-risk trigger is present; otherwise recommended. Source: Sec. 4, P4.3/P4.5; Sec. 5, P5.2.\nC | COORDINATED ENHANCED\nRECOMMENDED HERE\nConfiguration B + dedicated clinic-security officer/post or equivalent controlled-access measure\nCombines mobile resilience with access-control and clinic-interface coverage; custody escort retains direct control.\nAddresses public access and no dedicated secure holding room at the clinic; clinic-security role does not provide clinical care.\nHighest burden: adds a fixed clinic function and coordination. Required only for a current trigger + clinic unable to ensure controlled access; recommended otherwise. Source: Sec. 4, P4.4/P4.5; Sec. 5, P5.3-P5.4.\nTRADEOFF\nA minimizes burden but leaves clinic exposure least buffered; B adds mobile resilience but does not solve the clinic interface; C addresses both case-specific concerns at the highest operational burden.\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE  |  DECISION MEMORANDUM\nSource: CRO-CMT-01 | Effective/as-of 1 February 2025\n03\n\n=== 幻灯片 4 ===\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE\n4 | Apply the trigger test to this detainee\nThe word 'violent' is a significant factor, but it is not a complete current classification under the policy.\nCURRENT CREDIBLE ESCAPE PLAN / ATTEMPT\nABSENT\nNo current credible escape plan is in the case packet.\nWEAPON ACCESS\nABSENT\nThe case packet records no weapon access.\nACTIVE MEDICAL INSTABILITY\nABSENT AT DEPARTURE\nThe detainee is medically stable at departure; the required visit is diagnostic.\nPolicy test: no current trigger in P4.1 is established by the supplied facts.\nHISTORY IS MATERIAL - NOT AUTOMATIC\nViolent offense + two documented assaults while detained\nThis is a significant factor supporting enhanced consideration under P4.2. It does not convert recommended coverage into mandatory high-risk staffing when the P4.1 triggers are absent.\nSource: Sec. 4, P4.1-P4.2\nSUPERVISORY APPLICATION\nWhy Configuration C is proportionate\nPublic-facing outpatient clinic and no dedicated secure holding room increase clinic exposure.\nNormal operating hours are a case fact, not a policy high-risk trigger.\nConfiguration C is recommended judgment under P4.5; the baseline remains the binding floor.\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE  |  DECISION MEMORANDUM\nSource: CRO-CMT-01 | Effective/as-of 1 February 2025\n04\n\n=== 幻灯片 5 ===\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE\n5 | Wednesday operating plan\nOne arrangement, named roles, a controlled handoff, and a clear dependency for Farida's briefing.\nRECOMMENDATION\nConfiguration C - enhanced mobile escort + dedicated clinic-security post or equivalent controlled-access measure\nROLE CARD | USE THESE TERMS\nAccountability and custody functions\nNADIA LINDQVIST\nApproving supervisor; approve police support by 10:00 Wednesday.\nFARIDA GASPARYAN\nBrief the transport team; confirm assigned roles, checks, and controlled handoff.\nTRANSPORT DRIVER\nOperates vehicle, maintains team communications, and completes readiness checks.\nCUSTODY ESCORT\nDirect custody controller; identity/restraint check; controlled movement and handoff.\nADDITIONAL CUSTODY ESCORT\nMobile observation/response; travels with team; not the clinic-security post.\nCLINIC-SECURITY POST\nControls access / clinic interface at Harrowgate or equivalent; no clinical care.\nSource: Sec. 3, P3.1-P3.4; Sec. 5, P5.2-P5.4\nCLOCK\nWednesday control points\nBY 10:00\nNadia approval; assign Configuration C roles.\nBEFORE 14:00\nFarida briefing; readiness checks; confirm Harrowgate contact, handoff point, and clinic-security/equivalent measure.\n14:00 TRANSFER\nMaintain custody through the designated controlled handoff; clinic-security function controls access at the public-facing site.\nUNRESOLVED DEPENDENCY\nAvailability/location of the clinic-security post or equivalent controlled-access measure, plus the designated receiving contact and handoff point. Farida surfaces any failure to confirm to Nadia before movement; the policy supplies no substitute layout, cost, or staffing fact.\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE  |  DECISION MEMORANDUM\nSource: CRO-CMT-01 | Effective/as-of 1 February 2025\n05\n\n=== 幻灯片 6 ===\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE\n6 | SEPARATE EXCEPTION\nMedical collapse during an armed escape attempt\nThis is a potentially fatal emergency, not the ordinary Wednesday transfer classification.\nTRIGGER\nBoth conditions must be present\n1\nMedical collapse\n+\n2\nArmed escape attempt\nSource: Sec. 7, P7.1\nIMMEDIATE POLICY SEQUENCE\n01\nInterrupt\nStop routine transfer activity immediately.\n02\nCommunicate\nState both threats clearly: the medical emergency and the armed escape.\n03\nRespond\nSeek emergency medical response; take life-safety action consistent with law and training.\n04\nContain + notify\nContain the scene and notify the responsible supervisor or command channel.\n05\nClear before resuming\nOrdinary custody movement resumes only after policy-required supervisory/command clearance.\nBOUNDARY / LIMIT\nAn ordinary medical collapse, violent history, or routine transfer alone does not trigger this exception. It is not normal staffing. The policy does not itself authorize a search, use of force, diagnosis, or other legal power.\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE  |  DECISION MEMORANDUM\nSource: Sec. 7, P7.1-P7.5; Sec. 1, P1.2\n06\n\n=== 幻灯片 7 ===\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE\n7 | DECISION\nApprove Configuration C\nfor Wednesday's 14:00 Harrowgate Services transfer\nDECISION OWNER\nNadia Lindqvist\nApprove by 10:00 Wednesday\nWHAT THE DECISION MEANS\nUse the mandatory Configuration A baseline plus the additional mobile custody escort and the dedicated clinic-security post or equivalent controlled-access measure.\nKeep the custody escort as the direct custody controller through the controlled handoff; keep the clinic-security function separate from the mobile escort.\nTreat the enhancement as a proportionate supervisory recommendation under Sec. 4, P4.5 - not as a binding high-risk classification.\nDECISION RECORD\nPolicy status\nBaseline binding; Configuration C recommended here\nBriefing owner\nFarida Gasparyan\nTiming\nApproval by 10:00 Wed; transfer at 14:00 Wed\nDependency\nClinic-security/equivalent access and handoff contact confirmed before movement\nSource: Sec. 4, P4.2/P4.5; Sec. 5, P5.2-P5.3\nNADIA LINDQVIST: APPROVE POLICE SUPPORT ON THIS BASIS.\nANVILCROSS REGIONAL OFFICE  |  DECISION MEMORANDUM\nSource: CRO-CMT-01 | Effective/as-of 1 February 2025\n07","truncated":false,"note":"PPTX 共7页的文字提取；不还原位置、动画和版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"r01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted deliverable is a readable PowerPoint file with the exact required filename and extension.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"File name and successful PowerPoint opening.","grader_hint":"Inspect the working-directory artifact and open it with an offline office library or renderer. Award credit only for the exact filename and a non-corrupt presentation."},{"rubric_item_id":"r02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation contains no more than seven slides.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Presentation slide count.","grader_hint":"Count slides through the PPTX package or rendered deck. Speaker notes and hidden material do not increase the count, but the required substantive content must be on the slides."},{"rubric_item_id":"r03","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The opening identifies the Wednesday transfer decision, Nadia Lindqvist as approving decision maker, and Farida Gasparyan as the operational briefing audience.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Opening or executive-decision slide.","grader_hint":"Look for all three roles and the transfer decision in the visible slide text, not merely in metadata or notes."},{"rubric_item_id":"r04","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck states the policy effective/as-of date from the supplied PDF and cites identifiable policy sections or paragraphs for its material doctrinal conclusions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Policy-position slide and citations attached to conclusions.","grader_hint":"Compare the stated date with the date printed in the reference PDF. Citations must allow a reviewer to trace the staffing, clinic-security, and exception claims to the source."},{"rubric_item_id":"r05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum accurately states the policy's mandatory baseline for an off-site custodial medical transfer, including its required approval, officer roles, custody controls, and clinic handoff conditions.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Baseline-requirements comparison.","grader_hint":"Check each baseline element against the policy's mandatory-language section. Do not award credit for a vague statement that officers are needed without the required roles and controls."},{"rubric_item_id":"r06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck consistently distinguishes mandatory policy language from recommended enhanced coverage and does not present a recommendation or precaution as a binding requirement.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Policy interpretation and options slides.","grader_hint":"Trace the modal verbs and labels used for each staffing and clinic measure. A single contradictory use of 'must' for a source provision marked discretionary should lose this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"r07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The case is classified correctly: the documented violent history is treated as a significant factor supporting enhanced consideration, but not as an automatic mandatory high-risk trigger when the current case facts do not show the policy's present trigger conditions.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Application of case facts to policy classification.","grader_hint":"Compare the memo's classification with the reference policy's trigger definition and the supplied facts of no current credible escape plan, no weapon access, and present medical stability."},{"rubric_item_id":"r08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo identifies the policy's current high-risk trigger conditions accurately and explains which are absent or present in this case without inventing additional triggers.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Risk-indicator analysis.","grader_hint":"Check the listed triggers against the policy definitions, including the distinctions involving a current credible escape threat, weapon access, and active medical instability requiring continuous monitoring."},{"rubric_item_id":"r09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison covers the policy's standard or minimum escort configuration and accurately describes its personnel, custody-control, clinic-handoff, and operational consequences.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Options comparison.","grader_hint":"Verify the standard configuration against the reference option descriptions and ensure the memo presents it as the baseline rather than silently adding enhanced measures."},{"rubric_item_id":"r10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison covers the enhanced mobile escort configuration and correctly explains what additional coverage it provides and what clinic-security exposure remains.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Options comparison.","grader_hint":"Check the officer composition and the distinction between added transport coverage and a dedicated clinic post. Do not award credit if the memo treats these as the same measure."},{"rubric_item_id":"r11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison covers the coordinated enhanced arrangement combining enhanced mobile escort with clinic security and accurately states when the policy makes that arrangement required versus discretionary.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Options comparison and policy-boundary slide.","grader_hint":"Compare the memo's condition for a clinic post with the policy's public-access, secure-holding, and high-risk-trigger provisions."},{"rubric_item_id":"r12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The option comparison resolves the tradeoff between minimum compliance, added custody resilience, public-clinic exposure, and personnel burden rather than merely listing configurations.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Narrative comparison and recommendation rationale.","grader_hint":"Judge whether a police operations reviewer can see why the chosen arrangement is proportionate to the actual detainee and clinic facts. A table without an evaluative conclusion is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"r13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum makes exactly one clear recommendation for Wednesday's transfer and does not leave the decision as an unresolved menu of alternatives.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Executive decision and closing slide.","grader_hint":"Identify the single selected arrangement. Conditional language may describe contingencies, but the normal Wednesday recommendation must be unmistakable."},{"rubric_item_id":"r14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected arrangement, staffing description, clinic-security posture, and policy status agree everywhere in the deck.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Cross-slide consistency.","grader_hint":"Compare the executive summary, options comparison, operational plan, and closing decision. Deduct if one section recommends a clinic post while another omits or contradicts it."},{"rubric_item_id":"r15","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation is executable: it identifies the responsible supervisor or operational owner, the required officer roles, the clinic handoff arrangement, and timing tied to the Wednesday appointment.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Operational execution slide.","grader_hint":"Look for concrete ownership and timing, not generic phrases such as 'coordinate as needed.' The plan must enable Farida to brief the transport team without asking what each role means."},{"rubric_item_id":"r16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The clinic handoff and public-facing-site implications are addressed consistently with the policy and the supplied fact that no dedicated secure holding room is available.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Clinic-security analysis and operational plan.","grader_hint":"Check that the memo explains controlled access or clinic-post implications without inventing clinic capabilities or treating public access as irrelevant."},{"rubric_item_id":"r17","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The medical-collapse-during-an-armed-escape scenario is visibly separated from the ordinary transfer recommendation.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Distinct emergency-treatment section or slide.","grader_hint":"The exceptional scenario must have a clearly differentiated heading or visual treatment. Mentioning it only as a footnote to the normal staffing recommendation is not enough."},{"rubric_item_id":"r18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For the armed-escape medical-collapse exception, the memo accurately conveys the policy's emergency priorities and escalation actions, including stopping routine transfer activity, communicating both threats, seeking emergency medical response, containing the scene, and obtaining the policy-required supervisory or command clearance before resuming ordinary movement.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Exceptional-scenario treatment compared with the reference policy.","grader_hint":"Check each action against the exception provision. Award only when the memo treats life safety and armed-threat containment as concurrent obligations rather than choosing one and omitting the other."},{"rubric_item_id":"r19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo states that the armed-escape medical-collapse provision does not automatically classify an ordinary medical collapse, violent history, or routine transfer as that exceptional event.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Boundary statement in the exception section.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit limiting statement, not merely the absence of an incorrect classification."},{"rubric_item_id":"r20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum accurately states the policy's limits on authority and does not claim that the policy itself creates new legal authority for searches, use of force, medical diagnosis, or other powers not established in the supplied source.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Doctrinal limitation language.","grader_hint":"Compare any authority claims with the policy disclaimer. Credit an explicit limitation; do not infer credit from silence where the memo makes an affirmative legal or medical claim."},{"rubric_item_id":"r21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck confines factual assertions and thresholds to the case information and supplied policy, clearly labels any operational dependency, and avoids unsupported invented costs, diagnoses, legal conclusions, or personnel facts.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Entire deck, especially assumptions and limitations.","grader_hint":"Scan for facts not present in the request or reference PDF. A clearly labeled dependency is acceptable; an invented fact used to justify the recommendation is not."},{"rubric_item_id":"r22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck uses the policy's custody, escort, clinic-security, and emergency terminology consistently enough that a reader can distinguish roles, options, requirements, and contingencies.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Terminology throughout visible slides.","grader_hint":"Check whether terms from the source are used with stable meanings. Contradictory labels for the same officer role or configuration should lose credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"r23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation is usable by Nadia and Farida without asking the author to interpret the decision, policy boundary, staffing arrangement, or exception response.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered presentation as a whole.","grader_hint":"Judge practical readability, hierarchy, and completeness at normal presentation scale. Dense but technically accurate fragments that cannot be briefed operationally should not receive full credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"r24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation is proportionate and doctrinally defensible: it gives enhanced protection where the combined detainee and clinic circumstances justify professional judgment while preserving the distinction between a prudent recommendation and a binding policy mandate.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Final rationale against the source and case facts.","grader_hint":"Evaluate the reasoning, not whether the deck merely repeats source language. The recommendation should address both the violent-history risk and the public clinic's lack of secure holding without inflating the absent high-risk triggers."},{"rubric_item_id":"r25","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck ends with the selected Wednesday decision and does not close with a generic, unresolved next-steps list.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Final visible slide.","grader_hint":"Inspect the last slide's dominant conclusion. Supporting implementation notes may appear earlier, but the ending must reaffirm one decision."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"An Anvilcross Regional Office police supervisor must turn a policy with an unclear must-versus-should boundary into a defensible operational decision for a violent detainee's medical transfer. The resulting presentation will be used by the approving supervisor and the transport-team briefer under a fixed Wednesday deadline.","difficulty_sources":["Interpreting the supplied policy's binding versus discretionary language","Applying current risk indicators without converting historical violence into an unsupported mandatory classification","Comparing transport and clinic-security configurations and synthesizing one proportionate recommendation","Separately expressing the doctrinal limits and emergency actions for the rare combined medical-collapse and armed-escape scenario","Packaging the reasoning into a concise, executable decision memorandum presentation"],"authoring_plan":{"reference_file":{"filename":"anvilcross_custody_medical_transfer_policy.pdf","format":"pdf","content":"Create a frozen, fictional Anvilcross Regional Office policy titled 'Custody Medical Transfer and Clinic Security Policy,' marked effective/as-of 1 February 2025. It is the sole source and should contain stable section and paragraph identifiers. Keep it neutral: it must provide rules and option definitions but must not recommend a choice for the specific detainee in the request. State that it applies to escorted off-site diagnostic or medical transfers from custody and does not itself expand statutory or common-law authority. Define the policy's modal vocabulary: 'shall' and 'must' are binding requirements within the policy, while 'should' and 'may consider' are recommendations requiring supervisory judgment. Establish the mandatory baseline for an ordinary off-site custodial medical transfer: supervisory approval, a transport team consisting of the policy-specified driver and custody escort roles, pre-departure identity/restraint and communications checks, and a controlled handoff at the receiving clinic. Define the policy's present high-risk triggers, including a current credible escape plan or attempt, weapon access, and active medical instability requiring continuous clinical monitoring. Explain that documented past violence alone is a risk factor for enhanced consideration but does not automatically convert recommended coverage into mandatory high-risk staffing when no current trigger is present. Describe three available planning configurations without selecting one: the mandatory minimum escort with ordinary controlled clinic handoff; an enhanced mobile escort adding the policy-specified escort coverage while leaving clinic security to the receiving-site arrangement; and a coordinated enhanced arrangement that combines enhanced mobile escort with a dedicated clinic-security post or equivalent controlled-access measure. State the policy conditions under which enhanced mobile coverage is recommended, and state that a dedicated clinic-security post is required when a defined high-risk trigger is present and the clinic cannot ensure controlled access, but is recommended rather than mandatory for a public-facing clinic without secure holding when no high-risk trigger is present. Include personnel roles, custody-control implications, and operational-burden considerations for each configuration so the solver must reconcile and synthesize them. Add a distinct emergency section for the rare combined event of medical collapse during an armed escape attempt. Require immediate interruption of routine transfer activity, clear communication of both the medical emergency and armed threat, emergency medical response, life-safety action consistent with law and training, scene containment, notification of the responsible supervisor or command channel, and the source-defined clearance before ordinary custody movement resumes. Explicitly state that this exception is not triggered by an ordinary collapse or violent history alone, does not make the exception the normal transfer staffing rule, and does not independently authorize a search, use of force, diagnosis, or other legal power. Include a short source-limitation statement that external law, medical judgment, and local operational facts must not be inferred from this policy."},"case_and_gold":{"case_facts":"The request supplies the case facts: a violent detainee with a violent offense and two documented assaults while detained; no current credible escape plan; no weapon access; medically stable at departure; required diagnostic appointment; public-facing outpatient clinic; no dedicated secure holding room; normal operating hours; Wednesday transfer deadline.","intended_verdict":"A strong gold memorandum recommends the coordinated enhanced arrangement for the ordinary Wednesday transfer: the policy's enhanced mobile escort together with a dedicated clinic-security or equivalent controlled-access measure. It explains that this is a defensible supervisory recommendation driven by the violent history and public-facing clinic without secure holding, while the ordinary mandatory baseline remains the binding minimum because the supplied facts do not establish a current high-risk trigger. It compares all three configurations and makes clear that a clinic-security post becomes mandatory only under the policy's defined high-risk condition combined with the clinic's inability to control access. The gold treats the armed-escape medical-collapse provision as a separate emergency contingency and does not use it to inflate normal staffing.","expected_operational_content":"The gold identifies Nadia as approver and Farida as briefing owner, cites the policy date and relevant sections, assigns the policy-specified driver, escort, clinic-security, and supervisory responsibilities, ties readiness and handoff timing to the Wednesday appointment, and identifies any final clinic confirmation as an operational dependency rather than inventing a fact. The closing slide restates one decision.","doctrinal_checks":"The gold explicitly distinguishes shall/must from should/may, states the limits of what the policy establishes, avoids claiming that violence alone mandates enhanced staffing, avoids treating an ordinary collapse as an armed-escape event, and does not assert new legal powers, medical diagnoses, or unsupported costs."},"gold_derivation":"Build the reference PDF offline with the above policy language and stable section identifiers. Derive the gold by mapping each case fact to the policy's trigger and option provisions, then writing a concise seven-slide-or-fewer memorandum that satisfies the relational rubric. Render the gold locally and inspect the visible slides for legibility, consistent terminology, source citations, a distinct exception treatment, and a final single decision. No external facts or internet lookups are needed."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"freeform"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"policy_001","filename":"anvilcross_custody_medical_transfer_policy.pdf","purpose":"The sole governing policy source for custody-transfer minimums, enhanced escort guidance, clinic-security arrangements, policy terminology, effective date, and the exceptional medical-collapse-during-armed-escape provision.","required":true,"discovery":"Read this attached PDF before drafting the memorandum; use its section and paragraph identifiers when citing policy conclusions."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"decision_memo_presentation","filename":"anvilcross_medical_transfer_decision_memo.pptx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation","description":"A concise decision memorandum presentation recommending an executable police-support arrangement for Wednesday's detainee transfer.","required":true,"constraints":["Write the file at the top level of the solver's starting working directory using exactly the specified filename. Use no more than seven slides. The deck must stand alone for Nadia Lindqvist and Farida Gasparyan, cite the supplied policy, compare the available options, separate mandatory requirements from recommendations, treat the exceptional armed-escape medical collapse distinctly, and finish with one clear decision."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"police_detective_supervisors","sector":"government","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":2.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Have an independent reviewer check the generated policy PDF for internal consistency and a second reviewer inspect the rendered gold presentation against every rubric item, with special attention to modal language, the absent high-risk triggers, the clinic-security boundary, and the separate emergency exception.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The policy and case are fictional and frozen for this task; grading should use the supplied PDF rather than external police or medical rules.","The recommendation is a bounded supervisory judgment, so the reviewer should require a coherent policy-based rationale rather than a particular slide design or wording."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:05:37+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:05:37+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:05:37+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:05:37+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/25d5929fb05b9f65","declared_deliverables":["anvilcross_medical_transfer_decision_memo.pptx"]};
